# Snyk policy file — jikken
#
# Every entry below is a Snyk Code (SAST) finding that was triaged against the
# actual source before being suppressed.
#
# Reviewed: 2026-08-27. Expiries are set so each suppression is re-argued
# rather than inherited.
version: v1.25.0

ignore:
  # ── Use of Password Hash With Insufficient Computational Effort (CWE-916) ──
  #
  # Location: supabase/functions/jikken-simulate/index.ts — timingSafeKeyMatch()
  #
  # The rule fires because a fast hash (SHA-256, via crypto.subtle.digest) is
  # applied to a credential — the JIKKEN_API_KEY presented in the x-jikken-key
  # header. Read in context, this is not password hashing at all:
  #
  #   - The digest is never stored and never transmitted. Both digests are
  #     computed in-process, compared byte-by-byte, and discarded when the
  #     request ends.
  #   - It is the standard constant-time comparison idiom: hashing both sides
  #     to a fixed 32-byte digest before comparing means the comparison's
  #     duration does not leak how much of the key prefix matched. The loop
  #     runs the full digest length and accumulates with |= rather than
  #     short-circuiting.
  #   - CWE-916 is about work factor resisting OFFLINE brute force of a stored
  #     hash. There is no stored hash here, so there is no offline attack
  #     surface for a work factor to protect. Substituting bcrypt/scrypt/argon2
  #     would add per-request latency and defeat the constant-time property
  #     without removing any real risk.
  #
  # The expected key itself is read from a Deno environment secret
  # (Deno.env.get('JIKKEN_API_KEY')) — it is not hardcoded in the repo.
  #
  # This suppression covers ONLY this constant-time comparison. It would NOT
  # be valid for a fast hash used to derive or persist a stored credential
  # verifier; that remains a real finding. If timingSafeKeyMatch is ever
  # changed to store or return the digest, delete this entry and re-triage.
  'javascript/InsecureHash':
    - 'supabase/functions/jikken-simulate/index.ts':
        reason: >-
          SHA-256 here implements constant-time API-key comparison, not
          password storage. The digest is computed in-memory, compared, and
          discarded — never persisted or transmitted — so there is no offline
          cracking surface for a work factor to defend. A slow KDF would add
          latency and break the constant-time property without reducing risk.
        expires: 2027-08-27T00:00:00.000Z
        created: 2026-08-27T00:00:00.000Z
